::GEO_U02_PT_Q01::Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_PT_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule translates a point 5 units right and 2 units down? {
~A. (x, y) &rarr; (x - 5, y - 2)#This rule moves the point left 5 units instead of right 5 units. A translation right increases the x-coordinate, and a translation down decreases the y-coordinate.
~B. (x, y) &rarr; (x + 5, y + 2)#This rule moves the point right 5 units but up 2 units. The phrase "2 units down" means the y-coordinate should decrease by 2.
=C. (x, y) &rarr; (x + 5, y - 2)#Correct. Moving right adds 5 to x, and moving down subtracts 2 from y, so the rule is (x, y) &rarr; (x + 5, y - 2).
~D. (x, y) &rarr; (x - 2, y + 5)#This switches the horizontal and vertical changes and uses the wrong directions. Coordinate rules must match the stated movement: x changes for left or right, and y changes for up or down.
}

::GEO_U02_PT_Q02::Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_PT_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point A(-3, 4) is reflected across the y-axis. What is A'? {
~A. (-3, -4)#This changes the sign of the y-coordinate, which matches a reflection across the x-axis. A reflection across the y-axis changes the sign of x.
=B. (3, 4)#Correct. Reflecting across the y-axis changes x to its opposite and keeps y the same, so (-3, 4) becomes (3, 4).
~C. (3, -4)#This changes both coordinates. A reflection across the y-axis only changes the sign of the x-coordinate.
~D. (4, -3)#This swaps the coordinates and changes a sign. Reflections across an axis do not switch the order of x and y.
}

::GEO_U02_PT_Q03::Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_PT_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation preserves distance? {
~A. Dilation with scale factor 2#A dilation with scale factor 2 doubles distances, so it does not preserve distance. Rigid transformations preserve distance.
~B. Vertical stretch#A vertical stretch changes some distances by stretching the figure in one direction. Distance preservation requires the image to remain the same size and shape.
~C. Horizontal compression#A horizontal compression changes horizontal distances. That means it is not a rigid transformation.
=D. Rotation#Correct. A rotation is a rigid transformation, so it preserves distances and angle measures.
}

::GEO_U02_PT_Q04::Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_PT_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A point is reflected across the x-axis and then translated 3 units left. What should be done first? {
=A. Reflect across the x-axis#Correct. Transformation sequences must be performed in the order written, so the reflection happens before the translation.
~B. Translate 3 units left#This reverses the order of the sequence. In a transformation sequence, "and then" tells you what happens after the first transformation.
~C. Rotate 180&deg;#A rotation is not part of the stated sequence. Use only the transformations named in the instruction.
~D. Dilate by 3#A dilation is not part of the stated sequence, and dilation changes size. This item asks for the first step in the given sequence.
}

::GEO_U02_PT_Q05::Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_PT_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A figure has a line that divides it into matching mirror halves. What symmetry is shown? {
~A. Rotation symmetry#Rotation symmetry means a figure maps onto itself after turning around a point. The question describes mirror halves across a line.
~B. Translation symmetry#Translation symmetry means a figure can slide and match itself. A line of mirror halves describes reflection symmetry instead.
=C. Reflection symmetry#Correct. A line that divides a figure into matching mirror halves is a line of reflection symmetry.
~D. No symmetry#The description gives evidence of symmetry because the two halves match as mirror images.
}

::GEO_U02_PT_Q06::Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_PT_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.5<br><br>Question:<br>When drawing the image of triangle ABC after a transformation, what must be transformed? {
~A. Only vertex A#Transforming only one vertex does not determine the full image of the triangle. Each vertex must be transformed.
=B. Every vertex of triangle ABC#Correct. To draw the image of a polygon, apply the transformation to every vertex and connect the image points in corresponding order.
~C. Only the longest side#A transformation is applied to the points of the figure, not just one side. The image triangle depends on all vertices.
~D. Only the point closest to the origin#The origin is not a reason to choose only one point. Every vertex of the preimage must be transformed.
}

::GEO_U02_PT_Q07::Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_PT_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A translation maps one figure exactly onto another. What conclusion is supported? {
~A. The figures are different sizes#A translation preserves size, distance, and angle measures. If one figure maps exactly onto the other, they are not different sizes.
~B. Only color is preserved#Color is not a geometric property used to prove congruence. Congruence depends on matching size and shape.
~C. No mathematical conclusion is possible#A rigid transformation mapping one figure onto another supports a valid conclusion: the figures are congruent.
=D. The figures are congruent#Correct. A translation is a rigid transformation, and rigid transformations preserve size and shape, so the figures are congruent.
}

::GEO_U02_PT_Q08::Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_PT_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which triangle congruence criterion uses three pairs of corresponding sides? {
=A. SSS#Correct. SSS proves triangle congruence using three pairs of corresponding congruent sides.
~B. SAS#SAS uses two pairs of corresponding sides and the included angle. It does not use three side pairs.
~C. ASA#ASA uses two angles and the included side. It is not based on three side pairs.
~D. HL#HL applies only to right triangles and uses the hypotenuse and one leg. It is not the three-side criterion.
}

::GEO_U02_PT_Q09::Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_PT_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which information is not enough to prove triangle congruence? {
~A. SSS#SSS is enough to prove triangle congruence because all three pairs of corresponding sides are congruent.
=B. AAA#Correct. AAA can show triangle similarity, but it does not prove congruence because the triangles may be different sizes.
~C. ASA#ASA is enough to prove triangle congruence because two angles and the included side fix the triangle's size and shape.
~D. HL#HL is enough for right triangles when the hypotenuse and one leg are congruent.
}

::GEO_U02_PT_Q10::Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_PT_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.3<br><br>Question:<br>Why can valid triangle congruence criteria be connected to rigid transformations? {
~A. They always stretch one triangle#Rigid transformations do not stretch figures. They preserve distances and angle measures.
~B. They change corresponding side lengths#Changing side lengths would not preserve congruence. Congruence criteria rely on matching side lengths and angle measures.
=C. They provide enough evidence for one triangle to match the other by rigid motion#Correct. Valid congruence criteria give enough information to justify that a sequence of rigid transformations can map one triangle onto the other.
~D. They rely only on appearance#Triangle congruence is not proven by appearance. It must be justified using corresponding sides, angles, and rigid transformations.
}
